Article 15
(1) The Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), implementation shall be carried out by means of:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in both formal and informal education;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern towards the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every sub-district/village.

Article 21
(1) Regional Government develops cooperation patterns in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), is conducted between the Regional Government and:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Government;
d. Higher Education Institutions;
e. Social and Religious Institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2), includes: a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
(4) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 18
Recovery includes:
a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions; b. providing medicolegal services;
c. assisting the repatriation of victims;
d. providing temporary protection in safe houses (shelters); e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and
g. preparing the family, school, work and community environment, as well as economic empowerment.

CHAPTER X
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 23
(1) Regional Governments are obligated to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence. (2) Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. coordination;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.

Article 10
Every person who hears, sees or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obligated to make efforts in accordance with the limits of their ability to:
a. prevent and stop the continuation of acts of violence;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. assist in the process of submitting an application for the determination of protection for victims of violence.

